<!-- ── Insight ── -->
<div class="insight">
  <h3>Why the regression</h3>
  <div class="insight-grid">
    <div class="insight-item">
      <div class="il">QKV projection cost explodes when tiled</div>
      <p>
        Mega kernel eliminated the <strong>6.39ms</strong> external QKV projection block by fusing it into the RPA kernel.
        But doing QKV projection per <code>bq_tile</code> (128 tokens) means <strong>125 tiny matmuls</strong>
        of shape <code>[128×2560]×[2560×6144]</code> instead of one large
        <code>[16000×2560]×[2560×6144]</code>. The tiled path takes <strong>32.06ms</strong> for the same work —
        a 5× penalty from MXU underutilization and per-tile dispatch overhead.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="insight-item">
      <div class="il">FFN cost is unchanged — a clean control</div>
      <p>
        Both runs show <strong>exactly 19.36ms</strong> for non-QKV fusions (FFN gate/up/down + O_proj + element-wise).
        This confirms the profiler numbers are consistent and the regression is isolated to the fused QKV path.
        The mega kernel did not affect FFN or output projection at all.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="insight-item">
      <div class="il">Decode is unaffected</div>
      <p>
        <code>RPAd</code> shows <strong>≈1.0×</strong> in both runs (0.008ms vs 0.007ms).
        With 1 token per sequence, there's no prefill tiling — QKV projection is trivially small regardless of approach.
        The mega kernel advantage (if any) would only appear at <em>very small</em> prefill batch sizes.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="insight-item">
      <div class="il">Net accounting</div>
      <p>
        Mega saves <strong>6.39ms</strong> in external projections but adds <strong>+32.06ms</strong> to the RPA kernel,
        for a net regression of <strong>+25.67ms</strong> on the main pass. The 1.80ms increase in pad/broadcast ops
        is a secondary cost from per-tile x-staging inside the kernel.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

